\label{tab:robustness}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ remains robust: 8/12 under clustered SE, 7/12 under mixed effects, 5/12 under wild cluster bootstrap (Figure~\ref{fig:robustness}). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses under correction: OLS's 8/12 reduces to 0/12 (clustered SE) and 1/12 (bootstrap). Point estimates remain positive, but confidence intervals contain zero in 11/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:forest_plot}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_robustness_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Significance counts (FDR $< 0.05$) for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across four correction methods over 12 experimental conditions ($K{=}3$). Wild cluster bootstrap and clustered standard errors are recommended for the 15-judge combinatorial design.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure}

A power analysis (1{,}000 iterations, $\binom{15}{3}$ structure, 13 clusters per position) yields a minimum detectable effect of $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power, below the OLS estimate of 0.164. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s non-significance at $K{=}3$ is thus an \emph{informative null}: any true independent effect is smaller than 0.14 standard deviations. The sole negative coefficient (ARC $\times$ sycophancy, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$) does not survive correction (bootstrap $p \approx 0.06$). FDR counts are stable across $\epsilon \in \{10^{-3}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-9}\}$.

\subsection{Panel-Size-Dependent Regime Transition}
\label{sec:cross_k}

The $K{=}3$ results above show that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ fails to reach significance under corrected inference. A natural question is whether this negligibility is intrinsic to judge diversity or an artifact of small panel size. We address this by repeating the wild cluster bootstrap analysis at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels for each value and applying the same specification (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rrccc}
\toprule
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{Panels} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} \\
\midrule
3 & 455     & 5/12 & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ \\
5 & 3{,}003 & 0/12 & 7/12 & 7+/0$-$ \\
7 & 6{,}435 & 0/12 & 5/12 & 5+/0$-$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cross-$K$ wild cluster bootstrap summary ($B{=}9999$, Rademacher weights, $K$-position max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$, $\epsilon{=}10^{-6}$). As panel size increases, $\eta_{\max}$ significance drops from 5/12 to 0/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises from 1/12 to 7/12. All significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients are positive.}
\label{tab:cross_k}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_k} reveals a sharp regime transition between $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$. At $K{=}3$, $\eta_{\max}$ is the dominant predictor (5/12 significant) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is negligible (1/12). At $K{=}5$, this pattern reverses: $\eta_{\max}$ drops to 0/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises to 7/12, all positive. The transition persists at $K{=}7$ (0/12, 5/12). Diversity does not shift from harmful to protective across $K$; the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient is positive in every significant condition at every panel size. Instead, diversity transitions from a negligible to a significantly harmful predictor of panel vulnerability.

All significant $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients are positive across all $K$ values. The sole negative outlier (ARC $\times$ sycophancy, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$ at $K{=}3$) remains negative at $K{=}5$ ($-0.157$) and $K{=}7$ ($-0.176$) but does not reach FDR significance at any $K \geq 5$. The mechanism is tied to majority-threshold arithmetic: at $K{=}3$, a single compromised judge can flip the panel ($t{=}2$), making $\eta_{\max}$ the binding constraint; at $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise multiple judges, and panels with high $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ present more distinct attack surfaces. The attenuation from 7/12 at $K{=}5$ to 5/12 at $K{=}7$ reflects increased conservatism of the 7-position max-$p$ correction, not a weakening of the underlying effect.

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

We simulate non-adversarial noise by randomly flipping judge votes at observed per-judge error rates. Under random faults, both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ are significant positive predictors, surviving all corrections ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ bootstrap $p = 0.004$ and $0.019$; $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = +0.215$ and $+0.379$; $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$). Under adaptive attack, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s significance collapses (0--1/12 after correction): adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance beyond what panel structure explains. The contrast establishes that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association is genuine under random faults but inflated under adaptive attack.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_gam_keff_split.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ effect direction split by attack type at $K{=}3$ (GAM with FDR correction). Targeted attacks show positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity increases vulnerability), while shared-bias attacks show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity provides protection). At $K{=}5$, 11/12 conditions retain this pattern.}
\label{fig:gam_split}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Attack-Type Taxonomy}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM analysis reveals structure masked by the significance collapse (Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}). At $K{=}3$, the 12 conditions split 6:6 by attack mechanism. \textit{Targeted attacks} (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) show positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ in all 6 conditions: diverse panels expose more distinct weaknesses. \textit{Shared-bias attacks} (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias) show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ in all 6: diverse panels dilute shared susceptibility. At $K{=}5$, the split is largely preserved (7:5): one shared-bias condition (MMLU $\times$ sycophancy) flips positive, but the remaining 11/12 conditions maintain the targeted/shared-bias distinction.

\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.89 \\
Majority        & 11/12 & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.298 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 9/12  & 9+/0$-$   & 0.284 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 7/12  & 1+/6$-$   & 0.104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison ($K{=}3$, 15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}


The methods form a vulnerability gradient from threshold ($R^2{=}0.89$) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.104$; Figure in Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_details}). Bayesian calibration reverses the diversity channel: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction flips from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/6$-$). Threshold voting amplifies both channels ($\eta_{\max}$: 12/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: 11/12) because unanimity makes panels maximally sensitive to composition.

